Ask the clients to close their windows, and tell the application how many windows there are,
so that whoever runs the compositor can end an application politely and knows when its last
window has gone. Nothing happens unless asked for.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch and
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch in this directory, in that order. The lines of
context are from that project and from those patches.

It is kept small and in a shape that can be proposed to that project
(docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md). The rules are in one new file with its tests,
wayland-display-core/src/comp/windows.rs.

What it adds:

- A custom upstream event with a structure called `CloseWindows`, taken by the element
  `waylanddisplaysrc` the way it takes its input events (`KeyboardKey`, `MouseButton`, ...):
  the compositor sends `xdg_toplevel.close` to every toplevel, which is what the close button
  of a window manager does. What a client does about it is the client's business. One that
  has nothing to lose ends; one with unsaved work asks its user and stays. Nothing is closed
  by force, here or anywhere else in the compositor.
- A property, `window-messages`, off by default. With it on, the element posts an application
  message, `wayland.windows`, with a `count`: the number of toplevels the clients have, when
  the property is set and whenever the number changes. 0 says that the last window has gone.
  Popups and the cursor are not windows.
- The core has `WaylandDisplay::close_windows` and `WaylandDisplay::set_window_sink` for
  callers that do not go through the element.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`): one of the rule for telling a change, and four
with a real Wayland client: one that is asked to close and does (the count goes to 0), one that
is asked and does not (it keeps its window however often it is asked), two windows of which
one goes, and that nothing is asked or told unless somebody wants it.

Known limits:

- Every toplevel of every client is asked; there is no way yet to ask one window only.
- A client that never answers the request cannot be told apart from one that waits for its
  user. Ending it is up to whoever started it.
- Nothing is exposed through the C bindings yet.

Apply and build, in a checkout that has the first two patches applied:

    git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

